**Mixed anxiety and depression** is when you have symptoms of **both anxiety and depression**. Doctors sometimes use this name when neither set of symptoms is severe enough on its own to be diagnosed as a separate condition. It is one of the most common mental health problems in the UK.

Having "mild" symptoms does not mean they are not hard to live with. They can affect your sleep, work, relationships and daily life.

## Signs and symptoms

You may have some of these:

- feeling **low, hopeless or tearful** much of the time
- **losing interest** in things you used to enjoy
- feeling **worried, tense or on edge**
- **finding it hard to relax** or switch off
- **tiredness** and low energy
- **sleep problems**, such as struggling to get to sleep or waking early
- finding it hard to **concentrate** or make decisions
- **irritability**
- physical symptoms like a **racing heart**, headaches or an upset stomach

## What causes it?

There is usually no single cause. Things that can play a part include:

- **stressful life events**, such as money worries, losing a job, a bereavement or a relationship ending
- **long-term physical health problems** or pain
- **loneliness** and isolation
- **difficult experiences** earlier in life
- a **family history** of mental health problems
- **alcohol and drugs**

## Getting help

- **Talk to your GP.** They will ask how you have been feeling and may use short questionnaires.
- **Refer yourself to NHS Talking Therapies** if you live in England. You do not need to see a GP first.
- In **Wales**, ask your GP about your local primary mental health support service.

See [treatment for mixed anxiety and depression](/conditions/mental-health/mixed-anxiety-and-depression/treatment/).
